Yes. The interesting version isn’t really “suburban people with secret lives.” It’s a town composed of overlapping hidden games.

Every character has an ordinary public role—dentist, PTA mom, cop, realtor, pastor, contractor, councilwoman, teenager, bartender—but that is only one layer of their state. They’re simultaneously playing other roles that may not even have names:

* one person is building social debt,
* another is quietly deciding who belongs,
* two families are fighting a proxy war through youth sports,
* somebody is accumulating zoning-board influence,
* somebody is using gossip as reconnaissance,
* somebody thinks they’re having an affair but is actually being maneuvered in a business dispute,
* somebody is trying to become mayor,
* somebody else doesn’t care about politics but controls the church fundraiser, which accidentally makes them a kingmaker,
* and the guy obsessively maintaining his lawn may be playing a status game no one else even realizes exists.

The key is that the games cross substrates. Winning in one arena produces resources usable somewhere else. Friendship → information. Information → leverage. Reputation → votes. Sex → loyalty. Money → access. Access → narrative control. A kid getting picked as starting quarterback can alter the parents’ coalition structure six episodes later.

So the town itself becomes the protagonist: a dynamic network of alliances, debts, identities, rumors, status, attraction, resentment and institutional power.

And crucially, the characters have different levels of game-awareness. One person knows exactly what they’re doing. Another is consciously playing Game A while unconsciously playing Game B. Another sincerely believes they “don’t play games,” while everyone else can see that their need to appear above politics is itself an extremely effective political strategy.

That gives you a beautiful recurring device: periodically reveal that what appeared to be one scene was actually several simultaneous scenes.

A mother says, “Of course Emma can sleep over.”

Surface: arranging a sleepover.

Her game: repairing a friendship.

The other mother’s game: getting access to her husband, who sits on the planning commission.

Their daughters’ game: one is trying to steal the other’s boyfriend.

The husband overhearing it: realizes this puts his rival’s family inside his house.

The viewer suddenly sees five boards occupying the same kitchen.

You could even shoot scenes twice occasionally—not as a gimmicky unreliable narrator, but with a second pass that exposes a previously invisible game. Same words, entirely different meaning once another character’s objective becomes known.

The deeper rule could be:

Nobody possesses the whole game board because there is no single game board.

There are hundreds of partially coupled games whose moves sometimes coincide. When enough align, something that looks like fate happens: the sheriff loses an election, a marriage collapses, a development gets approved, somebody becomes town royalty, somebody gets socially exiled.

That also prevents it from becoming pure Machiavellian intrigue. People can genuinely love each other while also competing. Someone can help another person for five different reasons, only three of which they themselves understand. Cooperation, affection, altruism and friendship become strategies sometimes—but strategies can also become genuine relationships. The mask can grow into the face and the face can become a mask.

Tonally I’d put it somewhere in the space between Desperate Housewives, Big Little Lies, The Americans, Succession and a small-town political thriller—but without requiring murders or espionage to make the stakes interesting. The terrifying/funny revelation is that normal suburban life already contains enough game theory.

And there’s a very good escalation mechanism: begin on one cul-de-sac → neighborhood → school district → town → county → state politics/business/church/media connections. Characters discover that the little game they thought they were playing was merely a local node in somebody else’s much larger one.

A title in this world could be something deceptively domestic like The Neighbors, Good Company, Subdivision, Little Kingdoms, Everyone Knows Everyone, or my favorite structural title:

Side Games.

Because eventually you realize nobody knows which game is actually the main one.